## 2. The shared skeleton — why every module looks the same

Every business module is a 6-file NestJS unit:

| File | Responsibility |
|------|---------------|
| `*.interface.ts` | Domain types: `IXProfile`, `INewX`, `IUpdateX`, `IQueryXParams`, `IXEntity` |
| `*.dto.ts` | HTTP-boundary validation (class-validator) |
| `*.repo.ts` | Drizzle data access — the *only* layer that touches SQL |
| `*.service.ts` | Business rules + authorization |
| `*.controller.ts` | HTTP routing, extracts session → tenancy context |
| `*.module.ts` | DI wiring |

**Why this rigid uniformity:** the codebase follows a deliberate convention (following
the `ai-agent-cluster/backend` NestJS + Drizzle pattern). The payoff is that every
entity is learnable once and predictable everywhere, and cross-module imports are safe
because each module exposes its repo for others to consume.

### Three shared primitives that explain the "why" behind almost every method signature

**(a) `DefaultFields` / soft-delete (`common.schema.ts`)** — every table gets `id`
(internal serial PK), `slug` (public UUID), timestamps, and
`isDeleted`/`isActive`/`deletedAt`.

- *Why two identifiers:* internal `id` is used for fast FK joins; the `slug` UUID is
  what's exposed over HTTP so you never leak row counts or allow ID enumeration.
  Controllers resolve `:slug` → entity, then pass `entity.id` inward.
- *Why soft-delete:* this is an analytics/learning product — destroying history would
  defeat the purpose. The lone exception is `activity_event`, which is append-only and
  has *no* soft-delete columns at all.

**(b) `IDBConfigOptions ctx` threaded through every call** — produced by
`DbContextService.forUser(userId)`. It carries `{ database_uri, schema_id, user_id }`.

- *Why thread it everywhere instead of a global:* this is the **multi-tenancy +
  row-level-security seam.** Every repo call runs scoped to a company schema and an
  acting user. `system()` (user_id 0) exists for trusted internal operations. This is
  why no service method is "context-free."

**(c) CASL authorization (`assertAbility`)** — services that mutate take an
`ability: AppAbility` and call `assertAbility(ability, 'update', 'Task', entity, ...)`
*after* loading the row but *before* writing.

- *Why authorize in the service, not just a guard:* many checks are **row-level**
  ("can you edit *this* initiative"), which requires the loaded entity. Controllers do
  coarse `@CheckPolicies` (can you create *any* ActivityEvent); services do
  fine-grained per-row checks. **Caveat:** this discipline is applied unevenly — see
  §11 (issues #1, #2, #8). The role matrix lives in `casl/ability.factory.ts`:

  | Role | Effective ability |
  |------|-------------------|
  | `admin` | `manage all` — owns org structure and everything else |
  | `executive` | `read all` — read-only across the product |
  | `manager` | `read all`; `manage` KeyResult/Initiative/Task/Label/AlignmentLink; `manage` Objective they own or scoped to their team; `manage` Intervention they decided; `create` ActivityEvent as themselves |
  | `member` | `read` OKR/org entities; `manage` Initiative they own and Task they created; `create`/`read` their own ActivityEvents; `update` their own Person row |

