## 4. Layer 2 — OKR / Strategy

**Modules: `objective`, `key-result`, `initiative`, `alignment`**

### Objective — the aspirational goal

`title`, `ownerPersonId`, `period`, and crucially **`scope` (`org | department | team`)
+ `scopeRefId`**.

- *Why scope+refId instead of separate FK columns:* one polymorphic pointer lets an
  objective belong to the org, a department, or a team without three nullable foreign
  keys. `status` runs `draft → active → completed → archived`.
- `ObjectiveService` gates `update`/`remove` with row-level `assertAbility`
  (`update`/`delete` `Objective`). `findByScope(scope, scopeRefId)` powers scoped
  rollups. **Gap:** `create` does not validate that `scopeRefId` actually references a
  live row of the declared `scope` (the same polymorphic-integrity hole alignment had —
  see §11 #5).

### KeyResult — the measurable target

Belongs to an objective. Carries `metricType` (`number | percent | currency |
boolean`), `direction` (`increase | decrease`), and `startValue / targetValue /
currentValue` as **decimals**.

- *Why decimal-as-string:* financial/precise metrics; Postgres `decimal(20,4)` avoids
  float drift. The `direction` field is what makes progress math work for *both* "grow
  revenue" and "reduce churn."
- `updateCurrentValue` is the seam the tracking module writes through during a
  measurement. **Note:** `KeyResultService` mutations (`create`, `update`,
  `updateCurrentValue`, `remove`) carry **no** row-level `assertAbility` — they rely
  entirely on the controller's coarse `can(..,'KeyResult')`. Because only `manager`/
  `admin` get write ability on KeyResult, this is *not* a privilege escalation, but it
  is an asymmetry vs. Objective/Initiative/Task (§11 #8).

### Initiative — the unit of work that moves KRs

`title`, `ownerPersonId`, `priority`, `dueDate`, and a **denormalized `status`** cache.
Links to KRs via the `initiative_key_result` join table.

- *Why a denormalized status with a comment "authoritative status is the tracking
  projection":* this is the central architectural tension of the system — see §6. The
  initiative table holds a *cache* so simple list queries are fast; the *truth* lives in
  the event-sourced projection.
- `InitiativeService` gates `update`/`remove` row-level. `linkKeyResult` dedups via
  select-then-insert (no DB unique constraint → racy; §11 #6) and does not verify the
  KR exists (§11 #5).

### Alignment — the OKR graph

A separate module holding `alignment_link` rows:
`{ fromType, fromId, toType, toId, weight }`.

- *Why a generic edge table instead of FKs on objectives:* OKRs form a **directed
  graph**, not a tree — an objective can ladder up to multiple parents with a `weight`.
- **Edge-type integrity (resolved — was the §9 caveat).** `fromType`/`toType` are now a
  dedicated Postgres enum `alignable_type` (`'objective' | 'key_result'`), not free-form
  `varchar`. This matches the precedent set by tracking's `subject_type` enum and lets
  the DB reject typo'd/invalid edge kinds. The TS union `AlignableType` is derived from
  `alignableType.enumValues` (`alignment.constants.ts`) so code and DB cannot drift;
  `@IsIn([...ALIGNABLE_TYPES])` enforces it at the HTTP boundary. New linkable kinds are
  added with `ALTER TYPE alignable_type ADD VALUE …` — the additive flexibility the
  varchar was protecting, without the integrity loss. (Migration `0005`.)
- **Write-path guards (added with the fix).** `AlignmentService.link` now rejects
  self-links, verifies *both* endpoints reference a live row of the declared type
  (`objectiveRepo`/`keyResultRepo`), and rejects duplicate live edges. A **partial
  unique index** `alignment_link_unique_edge` on `(from_type, from_id, to_type, to_id)
  WHERE is_deleted = false` enforces no-duplicate at the DB level too.
- `OkrTreeService` is the brain here. Two notable pieces of real logic (rare in this
  otherwise-CRUD codebase):
  - **`computeKrProgress`** — pure, direction-aware progress %. Handles the degenerate
    `target == start` case explicitly (complete iff current already meets target) and
    clamps to [0,1] with a NaN guard. The pace heuristic is honestly commented as a
    **placeholder** because `period` is a label string (`"2024-Q1"`), not a date range —
    a known limitation, not an oversight.
  - **`buildTree`** — recursive graph walk with a **`visited` Set cycle guard** (A→B→A
    returns a leaf instead of looping forever). It calls `findChildren('objective', id)`
    (rows whose `to_*` side is this objective) and recurses only into rows whose
    `fromType === 'objective'` — that test is **load-bearing**, not redundant: a child
    can legitimately be an `objective` (ladder) or a `key_result` (alignment), and only
    child objectives form the recursive tree.

