## 11. Issues & logic bugs found (ranked)

> Severity reflects security/correctness impact in the current code. Each item cites the
> source so it's actionable.

| # | Severity | Issue | Where |
|---|----------|-------|-------|
| 1 | **High** | Tracking lifecycle has no row-level authz | `tracking.controller.ts` (all lifecycle routes) |
| 2 | **High** | Member ActivityEvent read scope not enforced on timelines | `tracking.controller.ts` `getTimeline`/`getTaskTimeline`, `activity-event.repo.ts` `listBySubject` |
| 3 | **Medium** | No lifecycle state-machine validation | `projection/*-state.projector.ts` |
| 4 | **Medium** | `time_logged` lost-update race | both projectors, `time_logged` case |
| 5 | **Medium** | Missing referential-integrity on cross-entity links | task/initiative/intervention/objective repos & services |
| 6 | **Medium** | Join tables lack DB unique constraints (racy app-level dedup) | `task_assignee`, `task_label`, `initiative_key_result`, `intervention_key_result` |
| 7 | **Low-Med** | Sub-task / department re-parenting has no cycle guard | `task.repo.update`, `department` |
| 8 | **Low** | Uneven authorization depth across services | key-result, label, person.remove |
| 9 | **Low** | `task.completedAt` column never written | `task.schema.ts` vs `task.repo.updateStatus` / projector |
| 10 | **Low** | `task` reuses `initiative_status` enum (coupled domains) | `task.schema.ts` |
| 11 | **Low** | `recordReason` supports objective/key_result but no endpoint wires them | `tracking.service.ts` vs `tracking.controller.ts` |

### Detail

**#1 — Tracking lifecycle endpoints lack row-level authorization (High).**
Every lifecycle route (`POST /tracking/initiatives/:slug/complete`,
`/tracking/tasks/:slug/cancel`, `…/start`, `…/time`, `…/measure`, …) is gated *only* by
the coarse `@CheckPolicies((a) => a.can('create', 'ActivityEvent'))`. The controller
resolves the subject (`resolveTask`/`resolveInitiative`) but never asserts row-level
`update` on it. Meanwhile `TaskController.update`/`InitiativeController` *do* enforce
row-level ownership. Net effect: a `member` (who may only `manage Task {
createdByPersonId: self }` directly) can **start, complete, cancel, or log time on any
task or initiative in the tenant** through the tracking endpoints — and because status is
authoritative via the projection, this actually changes others' work state. **Fix:** in
each lifecycle handler, load the subject and `assertAbility(ability, 'update',
'Task'|'Initiative', subject)` before emitting (mirror `requireBySlugForWrite`).

**#2 — Member ActivityEvent read scope not enforced (High/medium).**
CASL grants `member` `read ActivityEvent { actorPersonId: user.id }` (own events only),
but the timeline endpoints use coarse `can('read','ActivityEvent')` and
`listBySubject` returns *all* events for the subject regardless of author. So a member
can read everyone's events on any initiative/task they can see. Either the CASL rule is
too strict or the endpoint must filter — they currently contradict. **Fix:** decide the
intent; if timelines are meant to be shared, broaden the CASL rule; if not, filter
`listBySubject` by `actorPersonId` for non-privileged roles.

**#3 — No lifecycle state-machine validation (Medium).**
The projectors apply any event to any current state with a blind `switch`. There is no
guard rejecting illegal transitions, so `complete` on a `cancelled` task, `start` on a
`completed` one, or `block` on `not_started` all "succeed" and overwrite the derived
status. The event log records nonsense as fact. **Fix:** validate the current
projected status against an allowed-transition table before appending (in
`TrackingService`, inside the transaction), throwing on illegal transitions.

**#4 — `time_logged` lost-update race (Medium).**
Both projectors do `const existing = (read total); upsert(total = existing + added)` —
a non-atomic read-modify-write using a plain `SELECT` (no `FOR UPDATE`, no SQL-side
increment). Two concurrent time logs on the same subject at READ COMMITTED can both read
the same `existing` and one increment is lost. **Fix:** push the addition into SQL
(`set total_time_logged_minutes = total_time_logged_minutes + :added`) or lock the row.

**#5 — Missing referential-integrity on cross-entity links (Medium).**
The `alignment_link` fix added endpoint existence/liveness checks; the same hole remains
elsewhere, all writing bare integers with no FK and no existence check:
`task.create` (`initiativeId`, `parentId`), `task.linkAssignee` (`personId`),
`task.addLabel` (`labelId`), `initiative.linkKeyResult` / `intervention.linkKeyResult`
(`keyResultId`), and `objective.create` (`scopeRefId` not matched to `scope`). A
client can attach a task to a non-existent initiative, assign a ghost person, or scope an
objective to a team that doesn't exist. **Fix:** template the alignment guard
(`assertEndpointExists`) onto these write paths; for `objective` validate `scopeRefId`
against the `scope`'s table.

**#6 — Join tables lack DB unique constraints (Medium).**
`task_assignee`, `task_label`, `initiative_key_result`, `intervention_key_result` enforce
idempotency only via select-then-insert in app code; two concurrent calls can both pass
the check and double-insert. **Fix:** add partial unique indexes (filtered to
`is_deleted = false`) like the one added for `alignment_link`, and drop the app-level
pre-check (or keep it as a fast path).

**#7 — Re-parenting has no cycle guard (Low-Med).**
`task.repo.update` and `department.update` let `parentId` be set to any value with no
ancestor-walk check, so a node can become its own ancestor. `alignment` defends against
this at read time (`buildTree` visited set); task/department traversals (`findChildren`)
have no such guard, so a future recursive task/department tree builder could infinite-
loop. **Fix:** reject a `parentId` that is the node itself or one of its descendants on
update; add a visited-set guard to any recursive traversal.

**#8 — Uneven authorization depth (Low).**
`KeyResult` (all mutations), `Label` (all), and `person.remove` have no row-level
`assertAbility`, relying on coarse controller policy, whereas Objective/Initiative/Task/
Intervention/`person.update` enforce row-level. Not currently exploitable (the coarse
rules happen to restrict writers appropriately) but inconsistent and easy to regress.
**Fix:** either document the intentional asymmetry or add symmetric row-level checks.

**#9 — `task.completedAt` never written (Low).**
The `task` table has a `completed_at` column, but neither `TaskRepository.updateStatus`
nor the projector ever set it on a `completed` event. The column is dead. **Fix:** set
`completedAt = event.occurredAt` when projecting `completed` (and clear on re-open), or
remove the column.

**#10 — `task` reuses `initiative_status` enum (Low).**
`task.status` and `task_state.status` use `initiativeStatus`
(`not_started…cancelled`) rather than a dedicated `task_status`. Values happen to match,
but the domains are coupled — an initiative-only status added later would silently become
assignable to tasks. **Fix (optional):** introduce a dedicated `task_status` enum (mirror
of the alignment dedicated-enum decision).

**#11 — `recordReason` capability partially unreachable (Low).**
`TrackingService.recordReason` accepts `initiative | key_result | objective`, but only
the initiative reason endpoint is wired in the controller; objective/key_result reasons
can't be recorded over HTTP. Harmless dead capability. **Fix:** add endpoints or narrow
the signature to match the surface.

